    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "Phenylethylmalonamide, a neutral benzyl-containing amide, rapidly appeared in rat CSF with a CSF/serum ratio of 1.06, indicating ready BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "Remacemide, a benzyl-containing amide, was detected in human and canine CSF, confirming meaningful CNS exposure after systemic administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Compound 2 showed equivalent plasma and brain concentrations, directly supporting good brain exposure for a lipophilic benzyl-containing molecule.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "Penicillin G, an anionic benzyl amide, had CSF concentrations only 1-2% of plasma due to rapid outward CSF clearance; this contradictory evidence is mechanistically less applicable to the neutral, non-acidic query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Pass. The query is a neutral, low-molecular-weight benzyl amide of a 2-nitroimidazole; multiple high-transfer benzyl amide/related CNS-penetrant analogues show good brain or CSF uptake, while the main contradictory penicillin G data reflect organic-anion efflux not relevant to this neutral molecule. The physicochemical prior is moderate and consistent with meaningful CNS access.",
